  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` and it passes `typecheck`. I couldn't run `vite build` or load it in a browser, so I haven't seen a rendered frame; fog density, camera framing and shadow quality are chosen from reading the engine source. The forest is a 180×180 ground plane plus exactly 10,000 instanced objects: 8,000 trees (trunks and canopies) and 2,000 rocks. They come from three instanced nodes (cylinder trunks, cone canopies, sphere rocks), plus the one plane, so no per-object meshes. Placement uses a seeded mulberry32 generator (fixed seed) over a 50 m radius disc, so the forest is the same on every load. Each trunk, canopy and rock gets its own hex colour (browns, greens with some yellow tints, grey stones). The scene has one shadow-casting directional light, a neutral environment for fill, and exponential-squared fog (`effects.fog`, density 0.0045) matching the background colour. The engine's orbit camera mode doesn't move on its own, so each frame an `app.onFrame` callback calls `app.camera.setPose` to move the camera in a slow circle of radius 88 and height 56 (about 105 s per revolution). The framing is meant to keep the whole disc in view. The canvas is used as-is (`resize: false`, `pixelRatio: 1`). `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after the first frames have been drawn, and the engine's own `requestAnimationFrame` loop keeps rendering after that.",
